  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nAs of 3 AM Tuesday morning, a midlevel ridge axis is gradually \nshifting east across the MS River Valley as a shortwave trough \nejects across the northern Rockies. An attendant surface trough axis \nextends from southeast CO through northwest KS and into eastern NE. \nThunderstorms accompany much of the trough axis from far northwest \nKS into NE. As the shortwave trough continues to lift into into \nsouthern Saskatchewan and Manitoba, the surface trough will slow its \nsoutheastward progression. By afternoon the frontal zone is forecast \nto reside from southwest KS into central KS and northeast from \nthere. Weak convergence along the front should allow for isolated to \nscattered thunderstorm development by late afternoon. MLCAPE of 500-\n1000 J/kg and effective shear values less than 20 kt will support \npulse convection. Inverted-v soundings yielding DCAPE up to 1500 \nJ/kg will support downburst wind gusts near 60 mph. As far as \ntemperatures go, the airmass will remain unchanged ahead of the \nfront with highs surging into the low 100s across all of central, \nsouth-central, and southeast KS.\n\nA secondary midlevel shortwave trough will eject across the northern \nPlains late tonight, pushing the surface trough axis further \nsoutheast. Shower and thunderstorm chances will continue in the \nvicinity of the trough axis overnight time. Transitioning into \nWednesday, the exact timing of the front will be pivotal for high \ntemperatures Wednesday afternoon. Areas ahead of the front are \nlikely to surge near 100 degrees with post-frontal areas in the 80s \nor low 90s. The frontal zone should sweep across the entire forecast \narea by Thursday with high temperatures in the 80s for most \nlocations. As the frontal zone progresses across the area, shower \nand thunderstorm chances will increase as well. PW values may exceed \n2\" near and ahead of the front, suggesting heavy rainfall is \npossible. \n\nTransitioning into Thursday night and Friday, zonal midlevel flow \nwill become established across northern half of CONUS with the next \nshortwave trough emerging across the Pacific northwest. The initial \ncold front will slide well-southeast of the area with the next \nsurface trough developing across the high Plains. This will return \nsoutherly flow to the forecast area. WAA driven showers and \nthunderstorms are possible late Thursday night and Friday morning. \nTemperatures will surge back into the 90s areawide Friday afternoon \nahead of the next cold front. The cold front will sweep across the \narea Saturday with additional shower/thunderstorm chances as well. \nThis frontal passage will similar to Wednesday's with southerly flow \nquickly returning Sunday into Monday as another lee surface trough \ndevelops across the high Plains. The frontal passage that many \nKansans have been hopeful for looks to sweep across the area during \nthe Monday/Tuesday timeframe of next week. A stout 1025 mb post-\nfrontal surface high will usher in a taste of fall with temperatures \nin the 70s/80s!",
